<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-gauss-theorem">
<title>Gauss's Divergence Theorem</title>

<p>
The divergence theorem relates the outward flux through the surface
boundary of a 3D region to the triple integral over the volume of the
enclosed region.
</p>

<theorem xml:id="thm-divergence">
<title>Gauss's Divergence Theorem</title>
<statement>
<p>
Let <m>\mathbf{F}</m> be a vector field whose components have
continuous first partial derivatives, and let <m>S</m> be a piecewise
smooth oriented closed surface. The flux of <m>\mathbf{F}</m> across
<m>S</m> in the direction of the surface's outward unit normal field
<m>\hat{\mathbf{n}}</m> equals the integral of
<m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F}</m> over the region <m>D</m> enclosed by
the surface:
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-divergence-theorem">
\underbrace{\iint_S \mathbf{F} \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}}\,
d\sigma}_{\text{outward flux through the boundary } S}
= \underbrace{\iiint_D \nabla \cdot \mathbf{F}\,
dV}_{\text{triple integral of the divergence over } D}.
</men>
</statement>

<proof xml:id="proof-divergence">
<p>
(Write <m>\mathbf{F} = M\,\mathbf{i} + N\,\mathbf{j} + P\,\mathbf{k}</m>.
Both sides of <xref ref="eq-divergence-theorem"/> break into three
pieces along the three components, so it is enough to prove
<md>
<mrow xml:id="eq-divthm-three" number="yes">
\iint_S M\,\mathbf{i} \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}}\, d\sigma
\amp = \iiint_D \frac{\partial M}{\partial x}\, dV ,
</mrow>
<mrow>
\iint_S N\,\mathbf{j} \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}}\, d\sigma
\amp = \iiint_D \frac{\partial N}{\partial y}\, dV ,
</mrow>
<mrow>
\iint_S P\,\mathbf{k} \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}}\, d\sigma
\amp = \iiint_D \frac{\partial P}{\partial z}\, dV ,
</mrow>
</md>
and add them; prove the third, the other two being the same argument
with the axes renamed. <em>Step 1</em>: suppose <m>D</m> is simple in
the <m>z</m>-direction, as in <xref ref="sec-triple-case1"/>, with
floor <m>z = u_1(x,y)</m>, cap <m>z = u_2(x,y)</m> and shadow
<m>R</m>, so that its boundary is the cap <m>S_2</m>, the floor
<m>S_1</m> and the vertical wall <m>S_3</m> of
<xref ref="fig-divthm-proof-tikz"/>. Say why the wall contributes
nothing. On the cap, use <xref ref="eq-flux-param"/> for a graph to
find <m>\mathbf{k}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{n}}\,d\sigma</m>; on the floor the
outward normal points the other way. Write the flux as two double
integrals over <m>R</m>. <em>Step 2</em>: evaluate the triple integral
of <m>\partial P/\partial z</m> by integrating in <m>z</m> first, as in
<xref ref="eq-triple-iterated"/>, with the Fundamental Theorem of
Calculus on the inner integral, and compare. <em>Step 3</em>: say what
to do with a solid that is not simple in all three directions, and why
the cut surfaces cancel; <xref ref="fig-divthm-proof-video"/> shows
the idea.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,20);
\draw[gray!35] (0,16.5) -- (15,16.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,10) -- (15,10);
\draw[gray!35] (0,4) -- (15,4);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,19.7) {Step 1: on the wall $S_3$, \qquad on the cap $S_2$, $\mathbf k\cdot\hat{\mathbf n}\,d\sigma =$ \qquad on the floor $S_1$, $\mathbf k\cdot\hat{\mathbf n}\,d\sigma =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,16.2) {$\displaystyle\iint_S P\,\mathbf k\cdot\hat{\mathbf n}\,d\sigma =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,9.7) {Step 2: $\displaystyle\iiint_D \frac{\partial P}{\partial z}\,dV =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.7) {Step 3: general solids};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in four rows, for the contributions of the wall, cap and floor, for the flux of P k written as double integrals over the shadow, for the triple integral done in the z direction first, and for the extension to general solids.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</proof>
</theorem>

<figure xml:id="fig-divthm-proof-tikz">
<caption>
The solid of Step 1 of the proof of <xref ref="thm-divergence"/>: a solid
<m>D</m> that is simple in the <m>z</m>-direction. Its boundary is the cap
<m>S_2</m> where <m>z = u_2(x,y)</m>, the floor <m>S_1</m> where
<m>z = u_1(x,y)</m>, and the vertical wall <m>S_3</m> over the boundary of
the shadow <m>R</m>. On the wall <m>\mathbf{k}</m> is tangent, so
<m>\mathbf{k}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{n}} = 0</m>.
</caption>
<image xml:id="image-divthm-proof" width="65%">
<shortdescription>
A solid between two curved surfaces over a region in the horizontal
plane, with outward normals drawn on the top, the bottom and the side.
</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
A solid sits above a shaded region R in the horizontal plane. Its floor
is a gently curved surface and its cap is a higher curved surface; a
