<figure xml:id="fig-divthm-proof-video">
<caption>
The idea of the proof, without the algebra: divergence is flux per unit
volume, so chop <m>D</m> into little boxes and add the flux out of each
one. Every interior face is shared by two boxes and carries opposite
outward normals, so the inside cancels in pairs and only the flux through
<m>S</m> is left.
</caption>
<video preview="play-button.png" xml:id="video-divthm-proof-idea"
       source="videos/divergence_proof_idea_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>



<example xml:id="example-divthm-sphere">
<title>Flux Through a Sphere, the Easy Way</title>
<statement>
<p>
Use the divergence theorem to compute the flux of the vector field
<m>\mathbf{F} = x\,\mathbf{i} + y\,\mathbf{j} + z\,\mathbf{k}</m>
through the sphere <m>x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = 1</m>.
</p>
<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(Compute <m>\nabla\cdot\mathbf F</m>, and then the right-hand side of
<xref ref="eq-divergence-theorem"/> is a constant times a volume you
know. The field is shown in <xref ref="fig-divthm-sphere"/>.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,6);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3.5) -- (15,3.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,5.7) {$\nabla\cdot\mathbf F =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.2) {$\displaystyle\iint_S \mathbf F\cdot\hat{\mathbf n}\,d\sigma = \iiint_D \nabla\cdot\mathbf F\,dV =$};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in two rows, one for the divergence and one for the flux as a triple integral.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

<figure xml:id="fig-divthm-sphere">
