<example xml:id="example-divthm-sphere-zyx">
<title>Revisiting the Flux of <m>\langle z, y, x \rangle</m></title>
<statement>
<p>
In <xref ref="example-sphere-flux"/> we found the flux of
<m>\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = z\,\mathbf{i} + y\,\mathbf{j} +
x\,\mathbf{k}</m> across the unit sphere by parametrizing the surface,
computing <m>\mathbf{r}_\phi \times \mathbf{r}_\theta</m>, and
integrating<mdash/>a full page of work that produced
<m>\tfrac{4\pi}{3}</m>. Recompute this flux using the divergence
theorem.
</p>
<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(Compute the divergence; then <xref ref="eq-divergence-theorem"/>
with <m>D</m> the unit ball reduces the flux to a volume. Compare with
the long computation, and with <xref ref="fig-divthm-sphere-video"/>.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,6);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3.5) -- (15,3.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,5.7) {$\nabla\cdot\mathbf F =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.2) {$\displaystyle\iint_S \mathbf F\cdot\hat{\mathbf n}\,d\sigma = \iiint_D \nabla\cdot\mathbf F\,dV =$};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in two rows, one for the divergence and one for the flux as a triple integral.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

<figure xml:id="fig-divthm-sphere-video">
<caption>
The divergence theorem converts the flux integrals of
<m>x\,\mathbf{i} + y\,\mathbf{j} + z\,\mathbf{k}</m> and
<m>z\,\mathbf{i} + y\,\mathbf{j} + x\,\mathbf{k}</m> through the unit
sphere into triple integrals. Use it to check both of your answers.
</caption>
<video preview="play-button.png" xml:id="video-divthm-sphere"
       source="videos/divthm_sphere_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<example xml:id="example-divthm-paraboloid">
<title>Revisiting the Paraboloid, the Easy Way</title>
<statement>
<p>
Evaluate the surface integral
<m>\displaystyle \iint_S \mathbf{F} \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}}\,
d\sigma</m>, where
<m>\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = y\,\mathbf{i} + x\,\mathbf{j} +
z\,\mathbf{k}</m> and the surface <m>S</m> is the boundary of the
solid region <m>E</m> enclosed by the paraboloid
<m>z = 1 - x^2 - y^2</m> and the plane <m>z = 0</m>, this time using
the divergence theorem.
</p>
<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(In <xref ref="example-paraboloid-flux"/> this flux took two surface
integrals. Compute the divergence, then apply
<xref ref="eq-divergence-theorem"/> with the solid <m>E</m> described
in cylindrical coordinates. Compare with the two-piece computation, and
with <xref ref="fig-divthm-paraboloid-video"/>.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,9);
\draw[gray!35] (0,6.5) -- (15,6.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,4) -- (15,4);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,8.7) {$\nabla\cdot\mathbf F =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,6.2) {$E$ in cylindrical coordinates:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.7) {$\displaystyle\iint_S \mathbf F\cdot\hat{\mathbf n}\,d\sigma = \iiint_E \nabla\cdot\mathbf F\,dV =$};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in three rows, for the divergence, for the cylindrical description of the solid, and for the flux as a triple integral.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

<figure xml:id="fig-divthm-paraboloid-video">
<caption>
The flux of <m>y\,\mathbf{i} + x\,\mathbf{j} + z\,\mathbf{k}</m>
through the closed paraboloid-and-disk surface: the divergence theorem
replaces two surface integrals with one triple integral. Compare its
value with yours.
</caption>
<video preview="play-button.png" xml:id="video-divthm-paraboloid"
       source="videos/divthm_paraboloid_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

</subsection>

</section>
